[gd_scene load_steps=12 format=3]

; Yetannia's combat energy counter.
;
; The skeleton is the base game's scenes/combat/energy_counters/ironclad_energy_counter.tscn with the
; five orb layers swapped for our purple hue-migrated copies and the two ironclad-only VFX children
; removed. Notes on what had to stay exactly as vanilla:
;
;  * the root must carry NEnergyCounter.cs at its ORIGINAL path - that is the script that drives the
;    counter (it rotates %RotationLayers, flashes the burst and writes the "3/3" label), and it looks
;    its children up with %Layers / %RotationLayers, so both nodes keep unique_name_in_owner = true
;    and keep their names. The two-layer split matters: Layers holds the static layers, and the two
;    rotating layers live under Layers/RotationLayers.
;  * node order inside Layers is the draw order, bottom to top: Layer1, RotationLayers(Layer2,
;    Layer3), Layer4, Layer5.
;  * materials/ui/energy_orb_dark.tres and themes/kreon_bold_shared.tres are shared with the base
;    game's own counters, so they are referenced at their original res:// paths and deliberately NOT
;    copied into this pack.
;  * the two PackedScene children of the vanilla scene (scenes/vfx/energy/ironclad/
;    ironclad_energy_vfx_back.tscn and _front.tscn) are NOT instantiated - they are ironclad flame
;    effects. BUT the nodes still have to exist: NEnergyCounter._Ready looks them up with
;    GetNode<NParticlesContainer>("%EnergyVfxBack") / ("%EnergyVfxFront"), and GetNode THROWS when the
;    node is missing. Dropping them outright threw two errors per combat and aborted the rest of
;    _Ready, which skipped RefreshLabel() (the label sat at its authored "3/3" until the first energy
;    change) and left _hoverTip unbuilt. So two EMPTY NParticlesContainer nodes are kept in their
;    original positions - behind Layers and in front of it - and only the flame contents are gone.
;  * those two nodes are NOT merely present, they must be USABLE. NParticlesContainer's
;    SetEmitting/Restart loop straight over `Array<GpuParticles2D> _particles` with no null guard, and
;    NEnergyCounter.OnEnergyChanged calls Restart() on both whenever energy goes UP. Declaring the nodes
;    with only `script` left `_particles` null, which turned "52 skipped calls" into a hard
;    NullReferenceException that killed the run on the first energy gain. So each VFX node carries a
;    real (inert) GpuParticles2D child and the exported node path, which is exactly how the vanilla
;    ironclad_energy_vfx_back/front scenes do it.
;  * TWO gotchas in that, both of which cost a full QA round to find:
;      - the node type must be written as Godot's ClassDB name `GPUParticles2D`, NOT the C# spelling
;        `GpuParticles2D`. The engine does not error out on an unknown class; it logs
;        "Cannot get class" + "a placeholder will be created instead" and quietly substitutes a plain
;        Node, so the exported array holds a reference that is not a GpuParticles2D and Restart() NREs.
;      - `node_paths=PackedStringArray("_particles")` belongs in the [node ...] HEADER line, which is
;        how every vanilla occurrence writes it, not on its own line below.
;      - `visible = false` on the particle child is NOT redundant with `emitting = false`. Godot's
;        GPUParticles2D.Restart() flips emitting back to TRUE, and OnEnergyChanged calls Restart() on
;        every energy gain - so the authored `emitting = false` stops holding after the first one and a
;        default 1x1 white square would restart on the orb. Hiding the node is what actually keeps it
;        inert, and it does not affect Restart(), which is all the caller needs to succeed.
;  * the label outline colour is the one non-texture red in the skeleton, so it was hue-migrated the
;    same way as the layers: Color(0.3, 0.0759, 0.051) (hue ~6 deg) -> Color(0.136, 0.051, 0.3,
;    hue ~260 deg), otherwise a dark red halo would sit on top of a purple orb.

